\import{fnip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fnip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{Morris–Lecar nullclines}
\date{2026-11-26}

\p{In the Hopf column of \ref{fnip-0005}, the first component of the
velocity vanishes when
##{n=\frac{I_{\mathrm{app}}-g_{\mathrm{L}}(V-E_{\mathrm{L}})
   -g_{\mathrm{Ca}}m_{\infty}(V)(V-E_{\mathrm{Ca}})}
   {g_{\mathrm{K}}(V-E_{\mathrm{K}})},}
provided #{V\neq E_{\mathrm{K}}}. At #{I_{\mathrm{app}}=60} and at
#{I_{\mathrm{app}}=100} the numerator of that formula is nonzero at
#{E_{\mathrm{K}}}, so for those two currents the graph is the voltage
[nullcline](fnip-0014). The second component vanishes on the graph
#{n=n_{\infty}(V)}.}

\p{At #{I_{\mathrm{app}}=60} the two graphs meet at one point,
#{(V_{R},n_{R})},
the [equilibrium](fnip-0014) named in \ref{fnip-0011}. At
#{I_{\mathrm{app}}=100} they meet at one point,
#{(V_{\ast},n_{\ast})}.
Each value is the solution of #{n_{\infty}(V)} equal to the displayed
formula for #{n}. The solid curve in each drawing is the voltage
nullcline, and the dashed curve is #{n=n_{\infty}(V)}. The marked
point is the equilibrium. An arrowhead gives the direction of time
along a drawn [orbit](fnip-0015).}

